      "writtenOn": "2018-06-04T20:20:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e That\u0027s a feature of the Manager and how it uses Cache\nManager just calls Cache interface methods. I can\u0027t see any other use of a Cache interface, unless using non-deterministic cache keys by creating a random cache key each time. But that would defeat the purpose of cache, wouldn\u0027t it.\n\nACME certs are cached so that upon restart a manager reuses existing certs. The fact that you can run a hypothetical distributed managers fleet is a side effect.\n\nTo me it\u0027s stating the obvious: because Manager has a Cache field of type Cache, it may reuse the same instance:\n\n  type i interface {\n    hello()\n  }\n\n  // f can share the same i\n  func f(i interface{})\n\nNot sure it explains what I mean.\nI just think this new line adds nothing which isn\u0027t already described in https://golang.org/ref/spec#Interface_types.",
      "parentUuid": "0d4368d2_97dbede0",
      "revId": "a9b124ad0cf357e607d2178e528a368f45436bce",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cb0f1eb9_6c469873",
        "filename": "acme/autocert/autocert.go",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 103,
      "author": {
        "id": 11715
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-06-05T13:59:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The feature I\u0027m referring to is the ability to complete authorizations started by a different Manager, because HTTP and TLS-SNI tokens are not only cached in local state. I added specific tests for this behavior.\n\nJust because something is allowed by the language doesn\u0027t mean it\u0027s supported. We explicitly document everything that\u0027s safe for concurrent use, even if looking at the spec you can pass any object to two go statements.",
      "parentUuid": "1b42f8f1_9db59d5d",
      "revId": "a9b124ad0cf357e607d2178e528a368f45436bce",
      "serverId": "62eb7196-b449-3ce5-99f1-c037f21e1705",
      "unresolved": false
